 "askAi": {
  "question": {
   "en": "Why does a feather fall slower than a coin if g is the same for both?"
  },
  "lesson": {
   "en": "Gravity and falling: feather vs hammer"
  },
  "hints": [
   {
    "en": "Hint 1: List every force on the feather as it falls. Is gravity the only one?"
   },
   {
    "en": "Hint 2: Compare the push of the air with the weight, for the feather and for the coin."
   }
  ],
  "answer": {
   "en": "Both are pulled by gravity, and in a vacuum both accelerate at $g$. In air, the feather's air resistance is large compared with its tiny weight, so the net force $mg - F_{air}$ and its acceleration are much smaller than $g$. For the coin the air's push is negligible, so it falls at nearly $g$."
  },
  "offlineHints": [
   {
    "en": "Without air, everything falls at the same rate. A feather is slow only because air pushes up on it."
   },
   {
    "en": "A feather falls slowly because the air pushes up on it. This push is called air resistance, and it matters most for light, spread-out things."
   },
   {
    "en": "On the Moon there is no air: a hammer and a feather landed at the very same moment."
   }
  ]
 }
